wsi/metal, anv: Tile4 swapchain images in the IOSurface's VRAM (B580.kext SURFACE_VRAM2), on top of 0019 + 0020 + 0022 + 0023 + 0024 + 0025 (mesa-rel-pkg-cpu3)

Linux compositors take a tiled (modifier) swapchain from the client; on macOS the "compositor" is WindowServer's Metal
plugin importing the IOSurface's VRAM backing. Rendering into a linear image costs a lot on Xe2: Minecraft's last step,
a 2560x1302 RGBA8 -> B8G8R8A8 vkCmdBlitImage, takes 122 us into linear memory and 30 us into uncompressed Tile4.

- wsi_device::iosurface.gpu_memory_modifier(): the DRM format modifier every user of the IOSurface's GPU memory reads.
  When it is I915_FORMAT_MOD_4_TILED the swapchain images are created with that modifier (ANV picks the pitch), and
  wsi_memory_allocate_info carries the modifier, the pitch and the pages' row pitch to the driver. The IOSurface's pages
  (CPU readers) stay linear. Swapchain creation falls back from Tile4 to linear GPU memory to page copies.
- ANV: anv_wsi_iosurface_gpu_memory_modifier() answers Tile4 for B8G8R8A8 when the kernel reports
  B580_CAP_SURFACE_VRAM_LAYOUT = 1 (only once WindowServer imports with SURFACE_VRAM2, i.e. its plugin reads tiled
  backings); the import makes the backing with DRM_IOCTL_B580_SURFACE_VRAM2 and its layout (modifier, pitch, cpp,
  width, height, the pages' row pitch), and the kernel pages it off for CPU readers with XY_BLOCK_COPY_BLT Tile4 ->
  linear. The backing stays uncompressed (no CCS modifier).
- nvmtl_anv_surface_vram_stage_v1 (0014) takes version 2 requests: imports go through SURFACE_VRAM2 and return the
  backing's layout to the Metal plugin; a kernel without it (ENOTTY) has linear backings only. Version 1 callers (old
  plugins) only ever get linear backings: the kernel refuses them a tiled one (EBUSY), and they keep private copies.
- The FPS log says whether the swapchain renders Tile4 or linear.

